```rust
use serde_json::Value;

pub fn solve(input: Value) -> Value {
    let mut jobs: Vec<i32> = input.get("jobs")
        .and_then(|val| val.as_array().map_err(|_| ()))
        .and_then(|arr| arr.iter().map(|val| val.as_i32().unwrap()).collect())
        .unwrap_or_else(|| vec![].into_iter().collect());

    let quantum = input.get("quantum").and_then(|val| val.as_i32()).unwrap_or(0);

    jobs.sort_unstable_by(|a, b| b.cmp(a));

    let mut result = vec![];
    for i in 0..quantum {
        if let Some(index) = jobs.iter().position(|&x| x <= i) {
            result.push(index as i32);
            jobs.drain(index..);
        } else {
            result.push(0);
        }
    }

    input.insert("expected", result)
}
```

This function first extracts the jobs and quantum from the input JSON value. It then sorts the jobs in descending order. It then iterates over the range 0 to the quantum, and for each index, it checks if the job at that index is less than or equal to the current index. If it is, it adds the job index to the result and removes it from the jobs vector. If it is not, it adds 0 to the result. Finally, it returns the input JSON value with the expected result inserted.

Please note that this function assumes that the input JSON value is valid and contains the "jobs" and "quantum" keys. If the input JSON value is not valid, the function will panic.
